[25] proteins.tex is named for its application (proteins) but titled for its mechanism
     ("Gene duplication", label ch:duplication) — a filename/title mismatch that makes
     grepping by title miss the file. Same pattern: walks.tex -> "Search and connect"
     (ch:search), distance.tex -> "Boosting shortest path multiplicity" (ch:distance),
     projects.tex -> "Activity specialization" (ch:projects).


== CHAPTER 6 — PARTLY RESOLVED 2026-07-15 (communities.tex extended) ==

Three new exactly solvable sections added from the "wants to be broken" series:
  arXiv:2607.01967 (ring), 2607.03010 (pseudofractal web), 2607.05954 (diamond RG).
Sections 6.1-6.5 unchanged; 6.6-6.9 new; summary rewritten. Full detail of what
changed is in new_in_the_second_edition.log.

Fixed:
  [5]  for ch. 6: the chapter was entirely model-based with no exact theory; it now
       carries three solvable cases and a mechanism (log R / m -> ln K).
  [15] the even-odd alternation in the cycle-length table was "observed but
       unexplained". The (m-n) ln 2 law now supplies the frame: what matters is the
       edge-to-node surplus, i.e. the number of independent cycles. (The alternation
       itself is still not derived — see below.)

Corrected framing:
  * The 1st edition treats detection-method dependence as cosmetic ("the exact figures
    differ ... but the split is the same either way"). The pseudofractal shows plain
    vs degree-corrected differ by 25x on the SAME web and SAME partition (1095 vs 42),
    and reverse which cut is preferred. A caution is now lodged at the definition of
    r_kappa in 6.2 and collected in 6.9. This is progress on Outlook open problem #2.

New result introduced in the book (see new_in_the_second_edition.log for the full
statement and the author-facing note):
  * log R = (m_edges - n) ln 2 for the circulant ring, verified for c=1..5, with the
    label term proved exactly. Explains the paper's c=1 case as a knife-edge
    cancellation and corrects the paper's stated reason. Flagged in the chapter as
    going beyond the papers.

Still open in chapter 6:
  * The even-odd alternation in r_kappa vs cycle length is still unexplained.
  * The three uncaptioned `center` tables (item [6]) are untouched — they now sit
    alongside three properly captioned figures, which makes the inconsistency worse,
    not better. Worth fixing before the edition ships.
  * The numerical r_kappa values (81, 98, 108, 1090) and the exact ones (34, 42, 44,
    684, 1095) are not commensurable: the former are frequencies over random
    realizations, the latter Bayesian posterior weights on deterministic lattices.
    The chapter keeps them in separate sections and never quotes them side by side,
    but a reader could still conflate them. Consider a sentence saying so outright.
  * The solvable cases are deterministic lattices with the partition supplied by hand;
    no detection algorithm is run. Stated in 6.6, but it does mean the exact results
    bound the numerical ones rather than reproducing them.


== CHAPTER 7 — RESOLVED 2026-07-15 (distance.tex rewritten) ==

Source: newest version of the preprint at
  github.com/av2atgh/ramsey_netcom/tree/master/local_multi_path_2026
which is substantially ahead of what the 1st edition was written against.

Fixed:
  [3]  "This closing chapter" -> "This chapter".
  [14] The exponential law is still not derived (it remains open upstream too), BUT the
       chapter now has a real mean-field theory it did not have before: <mu> ~ c_2/e,
       with c_2 = <k(k-1)>/<k> the average excess degree. Derived in a calculation box.
  [18] No longer rests on the preprint alone: adds the exactly solvable diamond lattice
       (migdal1975, kadanoff1976) and three real datasets (snapnets, stark2006,
       cornellarxiv). Five new bib entries added to references.bib (now 55).
  [4]/[5] for ch.7 only: the chapter now carries four data figures and a real-network
       section. It previously had one schematic and no data at all.

Corrected errors the 1st edition actually got WRONG (not merely thin):
  * The old baseline claim -- "sparse random networks accumulate multiplicity only
    logarithmically" -- is false as a general statement. It depends on the degree
    exponent: constant for gamma>3, logarithmic at gamma=3, power law n^(3/gamma - 1)
    for 2<gamma<3. The old eq:path-baseline has been replaced by eq:path-const,
    eq:path-log, eq:path-power.
  * The old box gave the duplication-split exponent as gamma = 1 + 1/q. It is
    gamma = 1/q (upstream Appendix A, eq. gammaDS) -- which is also what projects.tex
    already said, so the 1st edition contradicted itself across chapters. Fixed, and
    the cross-reference retargeted from ch:duplication to ch:projects (DS is the
    projects-chapter model).
  * The old text said the deterministic pseudofractal "grows only log-linearly, like
    the shuffled baseline". Upstream now shows its randomized version follows a POWER
    law (alpha ~ 0.16) while the deterministic original is log-linear -- i.e. it falls
    BELOW its own shuffled counterpart, which is a stronger and opposite-signed point
    about randomness. Fixed.
  * LS(n,1)* does not creep up logarithmically; it SATURATES (gamma = 5 > 3). The
    figure now shows the points peeling away from the fitted logarithm.

Still open in chapter 7:
  * The exponential law eq:path-typeII is motivated (square-counting + compounding),
    not derived. This is open problem #1 in outlook.tex and is open upstream as well.
  * The modulation of <mu> by degree correlations (data below MF for disassortative
    networks, above for assortative) is reported from the real-network data but not
    calculated; the mean field knows only c_2. Flagged in the chapter text as an
    invitation, and in the upstream conclusions as "remains to be addressed".
  * Upstream's conclusions contain a typo the chapter deliberately does NOT copy:
    "For networks with assortative degree correlations, the average shortest path
    multiplicity is below the MF approximation. In contrast, for networks with
    disassortative degree correlations, the average ... is below the MF approximation."
    Both say "below"; the data show assortative ABOVE, disassortative BELOW. Worth
    telling Alexei so the preprint gets fixed.

Figure pipeline (new): figs/mkfigs.py + figs/data/*.csv, copied from upstream. The
figures are deliberately NOT identical to the paper's: grayscale (the book prints b/w),
panels regrouped for the 5x8 trim, Computer Modern maths. See the docstring in
figs/mkfigs.py. figs/multiplicity_diamond.py is upstream's exact-arithmetic checker; it
reproduces the diamond table from scratch and was re-run to verify eq:diamond-pole and
the kappa -> 1.576 prefactor before either was quoted.


== BUILD NOTES (not defects) ==

- `latexmk -pdf main.tex` exits 0 and produces an 84-page main.pdf.
- The only warnings are benign pdfTeX "destination with the same identifier (name{page.})"
  duplicates, from \pagenumbering{gobble} in the front matter.
- main.pdf IS tracked in git, so any rebuild dirties the tree even with no content change
  (embedded timestamps/IDs). After a test-only build: git checkout main.pdf
- At the 1st-edition commit the root .tex files are byte-identical to paperback_edition_1/,
  so any root-vs-paperback_edition_1 diff is exactly the 2nd-edition delta.
